{"parentUuid":"0e615c38-a956-4272-8754-4562de7c194f","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"b181e0eb-34fb-4b99-8009-91607af5aeb8","agentId":"a0d8d847e1065c5ed","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01KcCJVnqSpPbWLknr7FRug3","type":"tool_result","content":"diff --git a/morphoview/__init__.py b/morphoview/__init__.py\nindex 979add4..fddaec8 100644\n--- a/morphoview/__init__.py\n+++ b/morphoview/__init__.py\n@@ -18,4 +18,12 @@ wedge between the end-plates drawn rim to rim.\n \n The slices are drawn as a reader looks at them: the sagittal with the front to the left, the\n coronal and the axial with the patient's right on the left, the axial with the front at the top.\n+\n+The lines and frames the export writes (python -m morphometry run --lines) are carried onto the\n+X-rays FleXray renders from the same CT by morphoview.projection, FleXray's own camera, and\n+morphoview.shapes gives curves, planes, polygons and volumes that project through it; the research\n+note research_notes/morphometry/README.md says, in its section \"Carrying the lines onto FleXray's\n+X-rays\", how each is held to what FleXray renders.\n+\n+    python -m morphoview project --lines lines.csv --frames frames.csv --manifest manifest.json --file images/<stem>_v00.png --labels MASK.nii.gz --scheme enhance --out DIR\n \"\"\"\ndiff --git a/research_notes/morphometry/README.md b/research_notes/morphometry/README.md\nindex 33bca9b..2cd5a02 100644\n--- a/research_notes/morphometry/README.md\n+++ b/research_notes/morphometry/README.md\n@@ -193,6 +193,32 @@ On the phantom every line of L3 and its two discs is drawn, and each length's en\n - L5's interpedicular distance (23.4 mm) is drawn across the canal's middle front to back, behind the pedicles, where the trefoil canal narrows; in the canal's front half, between the pedicles, it is 27.6 mm wide. This is the construction the comparison faults at L4 and L5 (above), and canal-shape.csv measures it in three studies.\n - L5's pedicle widths (19.8 and 22.8 mm) and heights (33.3 and 18.8 mm) are drawn on islands that take in the transverse process's root, and its anterior height (37.3 mm) and wedge (19.2 degrees) on a body whose lower end-plate rises steeply behind; the L5 rows are to be read with that in mind.\n \n+## Carrying the lines onto FleXray's X-rays\n+\n+The lines export gives each measure's two ends and each vertebra's frame in the world's millimetres of the mask's affine. `python -m morphoview project` carries them onto a synthetic X-ray FleXray renders from the same CT, through FleXray's own camera (morphoview/projection.py), and writes lines2d.csv (each end's pixel, its depth along the beam, whether the render sampled it, its magnification and its place on FleXray's 256-pixel canvas, and the line's length on the render), frames2d.csv (each origin and the tips of its three axes drawn 10 mm long), column2d.csv (the centripetal spline through the origins in the levels' order) and VIEW.json (the view, where it was read, and the sha256 of the lines, the mask and the code). With the mask the lines were measured on, it measures how far each end and each midpoint lies from its level's shadow on the render, a disc's from its two vertebrae's.\n+\n+```\n+python -m morphoview project --lines lines.csv --frames frames.csv --manifest manifest.json --file images/<stem>_v00.png --labels <mask> --scheme enhance --out DIR\n+python -m morphoview project --lines lines.csv --view view.json --out DIR\n+```\n+\n+The first form reads the view from SchwingNet's render manifest (tools/flexray_render.py: the row's pose and isocentre, the blocks' detector and ray samples); the second from a file of the view's fields (morphoview.projection.View). A render of SchwingNet's realism programme (tools/realism.py) names no camera in its manifest; its view file is the row's alpha, y and z as rot_deg (alpha, 0, 0) and xyz_mm (0, y, z), the recipe's 1250 mm, 0.6 mm and 1024 pixels, and the CT's volume centre as the isocentre.\n+\n+FleXray 1.0.2 renders through nanoDRR 0.1.5, and its camera is three pieces of arithmetic, which projection.py copies with the files they come from: the pose (the ZXY Euler rotation, the source at the rotated offset from the isocentre, a fixed turn for the orientation), the intrinsics (sdd over the pixel spacing, the principal point offset from the centre) and one ray a pixel from the source to the pixel's centre on the detector, row by row. The world is the CT's nibabel affine, which a crop keeps, so a mask read with its own affine from the same scan, as morphometry reads it, shares it. What holds the copy to FleXray, in tests/test_projection.py against fixtures/flexray.json, which tests/flexray_record.py recorded from FleXray itself on the straight L2 to L4 phantom at 0.8 by 0.8 by 1 mm in three views (a lateral cone beam, an oblique cone beam with its principal point off the centre and pixels taller than wide, and a parallel beam from behind):\n+\n+- FleXray's camera matrices and the rays it casts through 41 pixels, the corners among them, agree with ours to 5e-13 mm under all three of nanoDRR's orientations, and come in FleXray's order of pixels.\n+- 95 beads of 2.5 mm radius on the ends of the phantom's lines, rendered by FleXray at 2000 samples a ray: each bead's centroid on the render lies within 0.21 px of its projected centre (medians 0.05 to 0.08 px), where a camera slipped by half a pixel misses by 0.5. A fresh render where FleXray is installed, posterior to anterior on a square detector of 256 pixels, as SchwingNet renders, puts all 95 within 0.16 px (median 0.05).\n+- The shadow of each vertebra, sampled here as nanoDRR samples a ray (500 points from the source to the detector, each the label of its nearest voxel), equals FleXray's support of that label, where its channel is above zero, pixel for pixel in all nine views and levels.\n+- Every drawn line's ends land within one pixel of their level's shadow in all three views. The exceptions are the places Eisenstein's lateral construction puts at the midline between the facets (the canal line's posterior end and the apex-to-apex line), which land on the shadow in the lateral view, where a lateral film shows them on the facets, and lie 3 to 7 px off it in the other two; and each disc height's midpoint, which lies 3 to 4 px into the disc space on the lateral render.\n+\n+FleXray's own label masks are its channels above 0.5, which bone thinner than about 9 mm along the ray at 1000 HU does not reach, so an end on a thin process can lie up to 20 px outside FleXray's mask while on its shadow; the landing is measured on the shadow, never the mask. FleXray's segmentation reads a render on a canvas of 256 pixels, zero-padded to a square at its centre and resized; the mapping here matches its content box in eight image sizes and carries a spot's centre where FleXray's resize carries it, to 0.002 px.\n+\n+The fluoroscopic look is SchwingNet's realism edit (the FluXray recipe, schwingnet.realism): it renders at 1024 pixels and edits at the same size, keeping the renderer's masks beside each image, so a line's pixel on the render is its pixel on the edited X-ray, and FluXray's stored copies at 256 pixels are FleXray's canvas, a quarter of the 1024 with no padding. How far the edit moves an edge it redraws has not been measured; the edited image's FleXray segmentation against the render's shadow would measure it.\n+\n+The shapes beside the straight lines are morphoview/shapes.py, each in the world's millimetres and each projected by the same camera: curves, open or closed, which project exactly, since a straight segment of the world is straight on the render, and are cut where they pass behind the source; arcs, circles, ellipses and centripetal splines sampled to 0.05 mm of the true curve; planes, fitted to points by least squares with their residual, with the angle between two, where a line meets one and the angle the render sees one at, and their patches; flat polygons; and volumes, whose projection is the length each ray runs through them, by a fine sampling that is the chord through their voxels or by FleXray's own, and their silhouettes. tests/test_shapes.py holds each to its geometry: the curves to their tolerance, a spline to the centripetal spans written again in Hermite form, a circle seen through parallel rays to the ellipse's area to 1e-4, a fitted plane to its points, a ball's path lengths to its chords and their sum to its volume to 0.005, and FleXray's sampling to nanoDRR's counted point by point.\n+\n+On the phantom the command takes 2 to 4 s a view with the mask, most of it the shadows (0.3 to 0.6 s a level); the 46 tests take about a minute (`python -m unittest morphoview.tests.test_projection morphoview.tests.test_shapes`), and Live, where FleXray and torch are installed, renders the views again.\n+\n ## The survey\n \n The 43 papers of the clinical reread (bordercall PR 21, one note per paper) gave the definitions: Panjabi's for the body, end-plates, pedicles, canal, processes and facets; Eisenstein's for the least canal depth, the greatest interpedicular distance, the mid-waist and his lateral-film construction, which puts the canal's posterior limit on the line from the superior articular process's apex to the inferior's inverted apex, opposite the upper half of the body; Genant's and Teijin's corner heights and ratios; Zindrick's pedicle chord; Meyerding's slip; Castellvi's 19 mm. Their means, 4,476 rows from 39 papers, are the paper tables' series.csv (bordercall branch claude/project-thread-adplcz at f067734), which `compare` reads.","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"324d8b35-8610-46e0-b97d-dc1859260236","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:11:01.782Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"f6a7f8d5-2441-45a5-b366-7e7b6241041f","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","root":"/home/user/bordercall","branch":"claude/project-thread-gv744i","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":3,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":6},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/bordercall","visibility":"unknown"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/bordercall","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template","visibility":"unknown"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/bordercall","visibility":"unknown"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/SchwingNet","visibility":"unknown"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","platform":"linux"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"0e615c38-a956-4272-8754-4562de7c194f","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","sessionId":"cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"zany-kindling-liskov"}
