{"parentUuid":"35175190-e464-402f-8f44-e47eb152744d","isSidechain":true,"promptId":"6e1af180-8c3d-4a38-afa9-78985af48754","agentId":"a0d8d847e1065c5ed","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01W6YnAEeA4FktzZGoseGAyt","type":"tool_result","content":" M morphoview/__init__.py\n M morphoview/__main__.py\n M research_notes/morphometry/README.md\n?? morphoview/projection.py\n?? morphoview/shapes.py\n?? morphoview/tests/fixtures/\n?? morphoview/tests/flexray_record.py\n?? morphoview/tests/test_projection.py\n?? morphoview/tests/test_shapes.py","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"d58ac7f9-3687-4a94-be03-4b3c9bad36f5","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:48:24.919Z","serverClassifierContext":{"request":"69f6f27c-2163-475f-83bb-5b370f71efbe","context":{"git_state":{"cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","root":"/home/user/bordercall","branch":"claude/project-thread-gv744i","default_branch":null,"status":{"clean":false,"counts":{"staged":0,"modified":3,"untracked":null,"untracked_normal":6},"porcelain":null,"truncated":false},"visibility":{"origin":{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/bordercall","visibility":"unknown"},"push_remote":"origin","remotes":[{"name":"origin","host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/bordercall","visibility":"unknown"}],"visibility_cache":[{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/symphony-template","visibility":"unknown"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/bordercall","visibility":"unknown"},{"host":"github.com","remote":"OpenSpineConsortium/SchwingNet","visibility":"unknown"}]}},"live_cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","platform":"linux"}},"sandboxed":false,"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"35175190-e464-402f-8f44-e47eb152744d","permissionDecision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reasonType":"classifier"},"userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","sessionId":"cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"zany-kindling-liskov"}
{"parentUuid":"d58ac7f9-3687-4a94-be03-4b3c9bad36f5","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"a0d8d847e1065c5ed","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgYvPWxontiiK4mTAbuGA","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Tag6zkE2btm8yMAjVScyz7","name":"SubagentHandback","input":{"message":"## Review of morphoview/shapes.py and the tests\n\nshapes.py does what it claims where it matters. The worst I found are three code problems, all low severity, and a set of test gaps: the tests would pass several wrong implementations, two of them about 5% to 18 degrees wrong on SchwingNet's own geometry. Nothing under /home/user/bordercall was changed; all my scripts are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/review-shapes/.\n\nThe mutants were run by mutants.py and mutants2.py in that folder against test_shapes (17 tests), and against test_projection's Shadows and Pieces where a mutant touched sampling.\n\n### Findings, most severe first\n\n**1. Test gap: `seen_at_deg` is only tested on the beam's axis.** shapes.py:317-321; test_shapes.py:194-199. CONFIRMED.\n- The only test puts the plane at the isocentre and takes its expected normal from `cam.ray_direction` itself. Nothing else calls `ray_direction` off the axis.\n- So a version using `camera.beam` instead of \"the ray through the plane's point\" passes all 17 tests.\n- On the realism geometry (sdd 1250 mm, 1024 pixels of 0.6 mm), 18 degrees off the axis:\n  - an end-plate facing the beam is truly seen at 72.00 degrees; the mutant says 90.00;\n  - a plane holding the axis direction is truly 18.00; the mutant says 0.00.\n\n**2. Test gap: fine path lengths are never tested on oblique rays.** shapes.py:448/452; test_shapes.py:236-253. CONFIRMED.\n- The ball test's cone spans about ±0.8 degrees, and in the volume-sum test every parallel ray is exactly sdd long.\n- So a fine sampling that takes every ray to be sdd long passes everything.\n- For a ball near the corner of the realism detector (18 degrees off axis), that mutant is 4.88% short (median).\n- The FleXray sampling is not affected: its test recomputes the ray length itself.\n\n**3. Code: `circle()` takes a negative radius and returns a triangle.** shapes.py:196-202 (with `segments_needed` 174-175). CONFIRMED.\n- `circle(c, n, -12)` gives 3 points whose sides lie 6.0000 mm off the circle, breaking \"the circle of radius\" and the 0.05 mm tolerance.\n- A radius of 0 also gives three coincident points.\n- `arc` and `ellipse` refuse their degenerate inputs.\n\n**4. Test gap: the merge's re-indexing of middle pieces is untested.** shapes.py:162-167. CONFIRMED.\n- The only cut closed curve in the tests has two pieces.\n- A mutant that drops `- starts[1]` on line 166 passes the suite. On a ring dipping behind the source three times, it gives pieces of 11, 7 and 3 points instead of 7, 7 and 7.\n- The code as written is correct: checked against a dense-sampling reference for 2 and 3 dips, open and closed, first point in front and behind.\n\n**5. Test gap: `Curve.resampled` never has its step checked.** shapes.py:116-125; test_shapes.py:135. CONFIRMED.\n- The test only checks that the length is kept, so a `resampled` that returns the curve unchanged passes.\n\n**6. Test gap: `Plane.patch` side lengths are not checked.** shapes.py:323-333; test_shapes.py:201-209. CONFIRMED.\n- The test checks the area and the first side's direction only. Swapping half_a and half_b passes (first side 5 mm instead of 8 mm).\n\n**7. Test gap: \"largest face-connected piece\" is not pinned.** shapes.py:380-390; test_shapes.py:276-289. CONFIRMED.\n- The test's two pieces are far apart, so a mutant using 26-connectivity passes. The project command does not use `largest=True`.\n\n**8. Test gap: `Curve2D.length_px` is untested.** shapes.py:79-84. CONFIRMED.\n- A version that never closes a closed curve passes.\n- `area_px` is tested only under parallel rays.\n- Neither is used outside the tests. I checked both are correct (a 20 mm square gives 160 px and 1600 px²).\n\n**9. Docstring: the bound \"within its tolerance times the magnification\" is exceeded off the axis.** shapes.py:8-9. CONFIRMED.\n- Off the axis the render enlarges a displacement across the ray by (sdd / depth) / cos θ, not sdd / depth.\n- An arc whose chords bow by the full 0.05 mm, 19 degrees off axis on the realism geometry, lands 0.1571 px off its true projection. The docstring's bound gives 0.1488 px (5.6% over); on the axis it is exactly 0.1488.\n\n**10. Code: comparing or hashing a shape raises.** The frozen dataclasses with array fields (Curve, Curve2D, Plane, Polygon, Volume) raise on `==` and `x in [...]` (ValueError) and on `hash` or putting one in a set (TypeError). CONFIRMED. Nothing compares them today.\n\n**11. Overstated claims, and minor unpinned details.** CONFIRMED.\n- README.md:218 says the tests hold \"FleXray's sampling to nanoDRR's counted point by point\". In fact the reference in `test_flexrays_sampling_is_nanodrrs_point_by_point` (test_shapes.py:255-274) is the module's own `inside` and `Camera.rays`, so it checks only the window of samples read. Agreement with nanoDRR is tested only at the silhouette level, by Shadows.\n- These mutants also survive: left-end sampling instead of midpoints, a full-voxel default step instead of half, and `Plane.fit`'s RMS taken over sqrt(N−3).\n- shapes.py:15 says \"unless corners are given\", but `patch()` takes no corners.\n\n### Checked and found correct\n- **FleXray sampling against FleXray's own `render_drr` in float64**, on a volume of one attenuation everywhere:\n  - the volume had an oblique, anisotropic, axis-flipped affine;\n  - views were AP cone (principal point off centre, non-square pixels), parallel and PA, at 500 and 123 samples;\n  - also the detector plane cutting the volume, the source plane cutting it under parallel rays, a shadow running off the detector, and a source 60 mm away;\n  - `path_length(n_samples)` equals the channel divided by mu to at most 3.6e-14 mm, with 0 differing silhouette pixels.\n- **Rounding:** grid_sample's nearest mode rounds half to even, as `np.rint` does.\n- **The fixture:** the README's \"pixel for pixel in all nine views and levels\" holds (0 px differ; the test allows 2). Live passes under FleXray (2 tests, 55 s).\n- **Curve tolerances:** arcs, circles (positive radius), tilted ellipses up to 200 by 0.05 mm, and centripetal splines (hairpin, 1000:1 spacing, zigzag, random sets) all keep 0.05 mm.\n- **Fine sampling:** no bias against the exact chord through the voxel cubes (mean −0.004 mm) at 11 degrees off axis.\n- **The rest:** `catmull_rom`'s pyramid, arc handedness, `Plane.fit`/`meet`/`angle_deg`, a non-convex polygon's area, `patch` without `toward`, and the volume's centroid, box and corners all match closed forms.\n- **flexray_record.py:** its camera and ray reads are right.\n- **Not reviewed:** projection.py and the project command, except where shapes or the tests rely on them."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":432,"cache_read_input_tokens":339695,"output_tokens":2725,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":0},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":432},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":2725,"cache_read_input_tokens":339695,"cache_creation_input_tokens":432,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":432,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_01Tag6zkE2btm8yMAjVScyz7":{"message":"## Review of morphoview/shapes.py and the tests\n\nshapes.py does what it claims where it matters. The worst I found are three code problems, all low severity, and a set of test gaps: the tests would pass several wrong implementations, two of them about 5% to 18 degrees wrong on SchwingNet's own geometry. Nothing under /home/user/bordercall was changed; all my scripts are in /tmp/claude-0/-home-user/cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f/scratchpad/review-shapes/.\n\nThe mutants were run by mutants.py and mutants2.py in that folder against test_shapes (17 tests), and against test_projection's Shadows and Pieces where a mutant touched sampling.\n\n### Findings, most severe first\n\n**1. Test gap: `seen_at_deg` is only tested on the beam's axis.** shapes.py:317-321; test_shapes.py:194-199. CONFIRMED.\n- The only test puts the plane at the isocentre and takes its expected normal from `cam.ray_direction` itself. Nothing else calls `ray_direction` off the axis.\n- So a version using `camera.beam` instead of \"the ray through the plane's point\" passes all 17 tests.\n- On the realism geometry (sdd 1250 mm, 1024 pixels of 0.6 mm), 18 degrees off the axis:\n  - an end-plate facing the beam is truly seen at 72.00 degrees; the mutant says 90.00;\n  - a plane holding the axis direction is truly 18.00; the mutant says 0.00.\n\n**2. Test gap: fine path lengths are never tested on oblique rays.** shapes.py:448/452; test_shapes.py:236-253. CONFIRMED.\n- The ball test's cone spans about ±0.8 degrees, and in the volume-sum test every parallel ray is exactly sdd long.\n- So a fine sampling that takes every ray to be sdd long passes everything.\n- For a ball near the corner of the realism detector (18 degrees off axis), that mutant is 4.88% short (median).\n- The FleXray sampling is not affected: its test recomputes the ray length itself.\n\n**3. Code: `circle()` takes a negative radius and returns a triangle.** shapes.py:196-202 (with `segments_needed` 174-175). CONFIRMED.\n- `circle(c, n, -12)` gives 3 points whose sides lie 6.0000 mm off the circle, breaking \"the circle of radius\" and the 0.05 mm tolerance.\n- A radius of 0 also gives three coincident points.\n- `arc` and `ellipse` refuse their degenerate inputs.\n\n**4. Test gap: the merge's re-indexing of middle pieces is untested.** shapes.py:162-167. CONFIRMED.\n- The only cut closed curve in the tests has two pieces.\n- A mutant that drops `- starts[1]` on line 166 passes the suite. On a ring dipping behind the source three times, it gives pieces of 11, 7 and 3 points instead of 7, 7 and 7.\n- The code as written is correct: checked against a dense-sampling reference for 2 and 3 dips, open and closed, first point in front and behind.\n\n**5. Test gap: `Curve.resampled` never has its step checked.** shapes.py:116-125; test_shapes.py:135. CONFIRMED.\n- The test only checks that the length is kept, so a `resampled` that returns the curve unchanged passes.\n\n**6. Test gap: `Plane.patch` side lengths are not checked.** shapes.py:323-333; test_shapes.py:201-209. CONFIRMED.\n- The test checks the area and the first side's direction only. Swapping half_a and half_b passes (first side 5 mm instead of 8 mm).\n\n**7. Test gap: \"largest face-connected piece\" is not pinned.** shapes.py:380-390; test_shapes.py:276-289. CONFIRMED.\n- The test's two pieces are far apart, so a mutant using 26-connectivity passes. The project command does not use `largest=True`.\n\n**8. Test gap: `Curve2D.length_px` is untested.** shapes.py:79-84. CONFIRMED.\n- A version that never closes a closed curve passes.\n- `area_px` is tested only under parallel rays.\n- Neither is used outside the tests. I checked both are correct (a 20 mm square gives 160 px and 1600 px²).\n\n**9. Docstring: the bound \"within its tolerance times the magnification\" is exceeded off the axis.** shapes.py:8-9. CONFIRMED.\n- Off the axis the render enlarges a displacement across the ray by (sdd / depth) / cos θ, not sdd / depth.\n- An arc whose chords bow by the full 0.05 mm, 19 degrees off axis on the realism geometry, lands 0.1571 px off its true projection. The docstring's bound gives 0.1488 px (5.6% over); on the axis it is exactly 0.1488.\n\n**10. Code: comparing or hashing a shape raises.** The frozen dataclasses with array fields (Curve, Curve2D, Plane, Polygon, Volume) raise on `==` and `x in [...]` (ValueError) and on `hash` or putting one in a set (TypeError). CONFIRMED. Nothing compares them today.\n\n**11. Overstated claims, and minor unpinned details.** CONFIRMED.\n- README.md:218 says the tests hold \"FleXray's sampling to nanoDRR's counted point by point\". In fact the reference in `test_flexrays_sampling_is_nanodrrs_point_by_point` (test_shapes.py:255-274) is the module's own `inside` and `Camera.rays`, so it checks only the window of samples read. Agreement with nanoDRR is tested only at the silhouette level, by Shadows.\n- These mutants also survive: left-end sampling instead of midpoints, a full-voxel default step instead of half, and `Plane.fit`'s RMS taken over sqrt(N−3).\n- shapes.py:15 says \"unless corners are given\", but `patch()` takes no corners.\n\n### Checked and found correct\n- **FleXray sampling against FleXray's own `render_drr` in float64**, on a volume of one attenuation everywhere:\n  - the volume had an oblique, anisotropic, axis-flipped affine;\n  - views were AP cone (principal point off centre, non-square pixels), parallel and PA, at 500 and 123 samples;\n  - also the detector plane cutting the volume, the source plane cutting it under parallel rays, a shadow running off the detector, and a source 60 mm away;\n  - `path_length(n_samples)` equals the channel divided by mu to at most 3.6e-14 mm, with 0 differing silhouette pixels.\n- **Rounding:** grid_sample's nearest mode rounds half to even, as `np.rint` does.\n- **The fixture:** the README's \"pixel for pixel in all nine views and levels\" holds (0 px differ; the test allows 2). Live passes under FleXray (2 tests, 55 s).\n- **Curve tolerances:** arcs, circles (positive radius), tilted ellipses up to 200 by 0.05 mm, and centripetal splines (hairpin, 1000:1 spacing, zigzag, random sets) all keep 0.05 mm.\n- **Fine sampling:** no bias against the exact chord through the voxel cubes (mean −0.004 mm) at 11 degrees off axis.\n- **The rest:** `catmull_rom`'s pyramid, arc handedness, `Plane.fit`/`meet`/`angle_deg`, a non-convex polygon's area, `patch` without `toward`, and the volume's centroid, box and corners all match closed forms.\n- **flexray_record.py:** its camera and ray reads are right.\n- **Not reviewed:** projection.py and the project command, except where shapes or the tests rely on them."}},"serverClassifierRequest":"dd17e485-5ed2-4fa9-b0c3-3ada5ca1c98c","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgYvPWbUetwLCf5No1u8p","requestedModel":"claude-opus-5-5","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"774af700-fc4c-4bc1-8f58-6003ede47b9c","timestamp":"2026-10-30T18:48:50.800Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"max","perTurnEffort":"max","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_projects","cwd":"/home/user/bordercall","sessionId":"cbdcd57f-bcf8-5a71-ab64-4f0b9fbdb74f","version":"2.1.314","gitBranch":"HEAD","slug":"zany-kindling-liskov"}
